// x-typecheck-generics-F2: two refusals that are right but report a false
// reason.
//
// 1. `?` on an operand whose type is an unbound cell (a parameter no caller
// types). Strip::of (graphix-compiler/src/node/error.rs:474) takes the error
// form without binding the cell, the result type is the cell itself, and the
// raised type (error.rs:1365) is the cell minus itself, the empty set, which
// fix_echain_typ (error.rs:619) reports as `expected error not []`.
//
// command: graphix --check design/review-2026-10-05/repro/x-typecheck-generics-F2.gx
//
// expected: refused with a message that names `x` and asks for its type
// (`|x: [i64, Error<`E>]| x?` is accepted, and so is `array::map(xs, |x| x?)`
// over a typed `xs`).
//
// observed: refused at `x?`: `expected error not []`.
//
// 2. `let f = |b: bool, x: 'a| -> 'a select b { true => x, false => 1 }`
// expected: a mismatch, as `|x: 'a| -> 'a 1` reports
// (`type mismatch 'a: unbound does not contain i64`).
// observed: `cannot infer a finite type here: unification requires a type that
// contains itself ...; declare a named recursive type and annotate the
// binding`. contains_mismatch (graphix-types/src/typ/contains.rs:418) takes the
// open rigid 'a inside ['a, i64] for a cycle (open_cell_reaches, :169);
// `|b: bool, x: 'a, y: 'b| -> 'a select b { true => x, false => y }` says the
// same while `|x: 'a, y: 'b| -> 'a y` gets the plain mismatch.
let f = |x| x?;
f(1)
